seg-0dfd1160c5d7 = Clock-log formats

# text
# c1: src/clock_log.rs
# c2: (time_s, phase_s)
# c3: tau0
seg-f52a62872493 = <c1/> reads a measured clock log into one normalised phase series: a uniform grid of <c2/> samples, with the sample interval <c3/> stated or found, every gap counted and the log's units named. Eight formats are read, each a documented subset. Anything outside the subset is refused with an error that names what was found, rather than read approximately.

# text
# c1: tau0
seg-dbdbfec37ab6 = <b>Abbreviations.</b> RINEX is the Receiver Independent Exchange Format. TICC is the TAPR (Tucson Amateur Packet Radio) time-interval counter. ADEV is the overlapping Allan deviation, TDEV the time deviation and MTIE the maximum time interval error. <c1/> is the sample interval in seconds.

# text
seg-1d5a6c2090af = Where a log can be used

# text
# c1: telecom-timing
# c2: [series.clock_log]
# c3: series.samples
# c4: series.csv_path
# c5: clock_log
seg-2a391645eeeb = <b><c1/></b>: <c2/> in place of <c3/> or <c4/>. The log's phase, in ns, is the time-error series checked against the masks, and the report gains a <c5/> block saying how it was read.

# text
# c1: [clock_log]
# c2: measured
# c3: input_sha256
# c4: scenario_hash
# c5: runs = 1
seg-4c2a67008451 = <b>The clock kind</b>: a <c1/> table adds a <c2/> block to the run: how the log was read, the SHA-256 of the table (<c3/>; the run's <c4/> does not cover the table) and of the phase samples, and the overlapping ADEV, TDEV and MTIE at octave averaging factors. It needs <c5/>. The simulated holdover is unchanged.

# text
seg-2f464fe709c9 = Without the table, both kinds parse, hash and report exactly as before.

# text
seg-c931c5ec2f4a = The formats

# text
# c1: rinex-clk
# c2: TIME SYSTEM ID
# c3: END OF HEADER
# c4: AS
# c5: AR
# c6: CR
# c7: DR
# c8: MS
seg-7f608c733407 = <b><c1/></b>: RINEX clock files, versions { "2" } and { "3.00" } to { "3.04" } (including the { "3.04" } nine-character name field). The header is read only for its <c2/> and is skipped up to <c3/>. Of the data records, only the <c4/> (satellite) and <c5/> (receiver or station) records of one clock, selected by name, are kept. Their first value field, the clock bias in seconds, is the phase. Continuation lines and the <c6/>, <c7/> and <c8/> records are skipped. Epochs are the record's calendar fields, as seconds from 1980-01-06 { "00" }:{ "00" }:{ "00" } in the file's own time system, with no conversion.

# text
# c1: timelab
# c2: tau0
# c3: time value
seg-0b758195e7eb = <b><c1/></b>: TimeLab-style ASCII phase or frequency logs, the plain-text form that TimeLab exports and its ASCII import and Stable32 read. There is one reading per line: either a single column at a uniform <c2/>, or two columns <c3/>. Columns may be separated by white space, commas or semicolons.

# text
# c1: #
# c2: ;
# c3: %
# c4: key = value
# c5: key: value
# c6: tau0
# c7: units
# c8: s
# c9: ms
# c10: us
# c11: ns
# c12: ps
# c13: data
# c14: phase
# c15: frequency
# c16: time_units
# c17: s
# c18: mjd
seg-a7ae9e712bea = Lines starting with <c1/>, <c2/> or <c3/> are comments. A comment of the form <c4/> (or <c5/>) sets <c6/> (s), <c7/> (<c8/>, <c9/>, <c10/>, <c11/> or <c12/>, for phase), <c13/> (<c14/> or <c15/>) or <c16/> (<c17/> or <c18/>, for the first of two columns).

# text
seg-84cf463d5e74 = One leading non-numeric line is taken as a column header.

# text
# c1: x[i+1] = x[i] + y[i]·tau0
seg-84f4b59bcdbf = Frequency data (fractional frequency) is accepted as a single column only. It is integrated to phase as <c1/> (NIST SP { "1065" }, { "2008" }, §{ "4.1" }).

# text
# c1: .tim
seg-8cc445eb84f3 = TimeLab's binary and <c1/> project files are not read.

# text
# c1: ticc
seg-3d8853fa9c92 = <b><c1/></b>: logs of the TAPR TICC counter (TICC Operation Manual, revision of { "23" } November { "2016" }, "measurement modes"). Every data field is seconds with { "12" } decimal places.

# text
# c1: <seconds> chA
# c2: <seconds> chB
# c3: chC
# c4: x[k] = t[k] − t[0] − n[k]·tau0
# c5: n[k]
# c6: tau0
seg-3af6e4e7bc73 = Timestamp-mode lines (<c1/>, <c2/>) and the TimeLab mode's pseudo-timestamp channel (<c3/>) are event time stamps. The phase of the selected channel is its time stamp minus the nominal tick: <c4/>, where <c5/> is the whole number of <c6/> since the first event. A missed event is a gap.

# text
# c1: <seconds> TI(A->B)
# c2: TI
# c3: tau0
# c4: tau0_s
seg-cecf9df4ff10 = Interval-mode lines (<c1/>, or any label starting <c2/>) are read directly as phase, one reading per <c3/>. The scenario must state <c4/>, because the line carries no time.

# text
seg-75301936698f = Period-mode and Debug-mode output is refused. A channel whose readings do not increase is reported as Period mode.

# text
# c1: #
# c2: <number> <label>
seg-5ec2509907dd = Lines starting with <c1/>, and lines that are not <c2/> (such as the start-up menu), are skipped and counted.

# text
seg-cb4b7062dd5a = linuxptp and chrony logs

# text
seg-c6c70813a988 = These five formats read what a running time daemon writes about its own clock. They are for checking a timing chain after the fact (a boundary clock, a grandmaster's PHC, a chrony client) with the same estimators as the other logs. They do not read a daemon's configuration, and nothing here talks to a daemon.

# text
# c1: ptp4l
# c2: phc2sys
# c3: ts2phc
seg-e1f85ff146d2 = <b><c1/></b>, <b><c2/></b>, <b><c3/></b>: the servo lines of linuxptp, each format reading only lines of its own program. A reading is a line of the form

# text
# c1: [seconds.fraction]
# c2: ptp4l[812]:
seg-e32842403b6b = The time stamp is the first <c1/> in the line (the daemon's monotonic clock). It is also read after a syslog prefix, where a process id such as <c2/> has no fraction and is not taken.

# text
# c1: offset
seg-72678cd6c03e = The phase is the logged <c1/>, in nanoseconds, converted to seconds. It is the daemon's measured time error of its slave clock against its reference, as logged: positive when the slave is ahead. It is the error left under servo control, not a free-running clock's drift.

# text
# c1: offset
# c2: master
# c3: eth0 master
# c4: [eth0]
# c5: CLOCK_REALTIME phc
# c6: /dev/ptp0
# c7: clock = "<that text>"
seg-f9080412572a = The words between the time stamp and <c1/> name the source (<c2/>, <c3/> for a <c4/> prefix, <c5/>, <c6/>). A log with more than one source needs <c7/>; the error lists them.

# text
# c1: s2
# c2: n_skipped_lines
# c3: s0
# c4: s1
# c5: s2
seg-5bef874341f2 = Readings before the servo first reports <c1/> (locked) are left out and counted in <c2/>: <c3/> and <c4/> are the unlocked and step states, whose offsets are pull-in transients. A servo that never reaches <c5/> is refused. A line with no state field is read as it is.

# text
# c1: rms ... max ...
# c2: delay
# c3: selected best master
seg-121fbe1486cf = Summary lines (<c1/>), <c2/> and <c3/> messages, and lines of other programs are skipped and counted.

# text
seg-92164f4a56be = Time stamps that go backwards (a restarted daemon) are refused; split the log.

# text
# c1: chrony-tracking
# c2: chrony-statistics
# c3: log tracking
# c4: log statistics
# c5: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
# c6: ====
seg-d86fd2a815d8 = <b><c1/></b>, <b><c2/></b>: chrony's <c3/> and <c4/> files, read by column position. A reading is a line with at least { "14" } columns (tracking) or { "13" } (statistics) that starts with a <c5/> UTC date and time. The column header line is skipped and counted; the <c6/> rule under it is skipped without being counted.

# text
# c1: clock
# c2: clock = "<id>"
seg-bae4f6895d14 = Tracking: column { "7" }, the local offset on the clock update, in seconds. The IP address or reference id changes with the selected source, so without <c1/> every line is read as one local clock. <c2/> keeps one reference id.

# text
# c1: clock = "<address>"
seg-12b284cd7d8c = Statistics: column { "5" }, the estimated offset of a source, in seconds. There is one series per source address, and the log needs <c1/> when it holds more than one.

# text
# c1: tau0
# c2: tau0
# c3: n_duplicates
# c4: gaps = "refuse"
# c5: tau0_s
# c6: gaps = "linear"
seg-197645e3c7c5 = Epochs are seconds from 1980-01-06 { "00" }:{ "00" }:{ "00" } UTC, with no leap-second handling. Chrony writes a line per update or per source sample, so the spacing is not uniform. <c1/> is the median spacing; a reading under half a <c2/> after the one kept before it is dropped and counted in <c3/>, and a longer spacing is a gap, which <c4/> reports. Give <c5/> and <c6/> for a log with a steady poll interval.

# text
# c1: tests/fixtures/clock_log/linuxptp_chrony/
# c2: NOTICE.md
seg-61fbb0479c95 = The chrony columns and the linuxptp line layouts are those of the daemons' public documentation. The fixtures in <c1/> are written for Kshana with made-up values (see its <c2/>); no real log was read, so a layout that differs between daemon versions can still be refused or misread. A line that does not match is skipped and counted, never guessed.

# text
# c1: tau0
seg-b120a37b7d60 = <c1/> and gaps

# text
# c1: tau0
# c2: tau0
# c3: median-spacing
# c4: tau0_s
# c5: input
# c6: tau0
# c7: header
seg-6fd68aad2fd7 = When the log carries time stamps and no <c1/> is given, <c2/> is the median spacing of consecutive time stamps, rounded to four significant figures, and the source is reported as <c3/>. A counter's spacing differs from nominal by the reference's frequency offset, parts in { "10" }^{ "6" } or less. A <c4/> in the scenario always wins (source <c5/>), then a <c6/> header comment (source <c7/>).

# text
# c1: t0 + i·tau0
# c2: tau0
seg-59e52611a7d3 = Samples are placed on the grid <c1/>, and a missing epoch is a gap. Readings more than { "1" } % of <c2/> off the grid are dropped and counted, as are duplicates (the first is kept). The gap policy is one of:

# text
# c1: refuse
seg-61b1664a7c48 = <c1/> (the default): any gap is an error that counts the gaps;

# text
# c1: longest
seg-593c998b65e0 = <c1/>: keep the longest gap-free run;

# text
# c1: linear
seg-f184ce746389 = <c1/>: fill interior gaps by linear interpolation, and report the number filled.

# text
seg-1f3dc3429615 = The estimators need a gap-free record, which is why gaps are resolved here.

# text
# c1: src/clock_log.rs
# c2: tests/fixtures/clock_log/linuxptp_chrony/
# c3: tests/clock_log_allantools.rs
# c4: tests/fixtures/clock_log/PREREGISTRATION.md
seg-fe7d27a473aa = MODELLED. This is a parser, and its output feeds estimators that are validated elsewhere against allantools. The unit tests in <c1/> use small hand-written logs, and for the linuxptp and chrony formats the fixtures in <c2/>. <c3/> runs the comparison pre-registered in <c4/>: three seeded synthetic logs, one per format, read back by an independent reader written from this page by the same author, with the ADEV, TDEV and MTIE checked against allantools { "2024.6" }; that comparison covers the first three formats only, and the linuxptp and chrony readers have parser tests but no oracle comparison. The logs are synthetic, so nothing here describes a real clock or counter.

